#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105691 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "4b8266ba-b293-541f-9e4f-5005a6184816" :question/math-academy-id "e-9566" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" :question/worked-solution "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](images/03/03d1cadde8d86003bf86cbde2609b841731c55966fbdaa2b26988c2401ccf049.svg)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/6a/6a6fb806e99ba4cddc944ebed697bc4ddcee2569d63d8ecae8dee8d5f1fc05c8.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](images/e5/e5b7b74863ccacf150b132919751c4314784e0bf01763d0e1a1cf2df0de5ce62.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105692 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "ad8da3b7-7e57-58bb-a0d2-8cdbddf80239" :question/math-academy-id "e-9571" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/a6/a62b446c9a1b82ffd922f5708e84d12314afa02e9f7bdfe5e61f7d96670f1cea.svg)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/98/98bf5b8b1812e95d5d749fa12292329b1a2ead3f7d1e73c8b9bde767c4535d9a.svg)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](images/c2/c22e707e65334af655da1fb796fc1eae7665d9d56dd196282b3810941b450c3b.svg)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf4ba5d-ef9f-5153-ae1c-e4c0c5782ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420280} {:answer/id #uuid "20fcf7c1-0e53-5bc4-9774-94258e3e649e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420281} {:answer/id #uuid "6113e78e-3c31-5b91-be16-7271edc5b671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420286} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fbf14d-6a97-5982-90b0-30f215c18cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420292} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e65222-2412-514f-8cd8-601d4bfe9e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420295}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6113e78e-3c31-5b91-be16-7271edc5b671" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420286} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8d3a0d-de2c-5e6c-a792-1bd70490a272" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b936ee5c-7e32-56c6-afd0-2cda76d77856" :question/math-academy-id "q-126914" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{11\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105855 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f59392c4-996f-5b29-9c37-7a1b75ffd896" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Positively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4273bc0b-c236-5aef-b7ab-73105db3d922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420283} {:answer/id #uuid "77b52dfd-4977-5787-b514-fbe4937870e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420289} {:answer/id #uuid "a164f912-bf55-5a5b-ae4c-d8d627540a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420291} {:answer/id #uuid "d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420293} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b60cba-f8e0-5442-8c96-6db838819306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d905a5f3-7922-5213-884e-760e5bcf3649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420293} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5ad3f1-9a81-57d1-9a87-dc11af7a74bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f0ef420-2cbc-5713-b114-6ede5820bdae" :question/math-academy-id "q-126924" :question/problem "The closed, positively oriented curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ae1d17-586d-5656-9819-82e44f1f99e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420278} {:answer/id #uuid "3f50700d-4949-56a2-a27b-18e517742a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420282} {:answer/id #uuid "6627b401-f702-5235-8c84-b1a54bb3416f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420287} {:answer/id #uuid "672c97f7-89dc-5157-8a28-9704883db291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420288} {:answer/id #uuid "f95a11de-5734-527e-9084-6f608d51df00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f95a11de-5734-527e-9084-6f608d51df00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420296} :answer-field/id #uuid "3706a6bf-e668-594b-b09d-444f45050912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9ddb293-a141-5410-8588-0be8dbbb83c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-126928" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+14\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad522514-328e-5944-b4c7-91d7e3b302f0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vector for a Negatively Oriented Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0437d5f4-9256-56dd-87fb-59350017483d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420277} {:answer/id #uuid "16e038fe-7a43-52bb-9c39-90c0889e7847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420279} {:answer/id #uuid "439e9a02-8366-5fd0-88a4-aac15fafa034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420284} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1f89cf-4b72-5a63-9cc0-b059a94c4c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420285} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2f1542-170e-55d7-a28b-44d4c1502880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "439e9a02-8366-5fd0-88a4-aac15fafa034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420284} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e6414f4-2314-56d1-97b2-1f02e11d8c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420299}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97a1a962-e2cb-547c-a9e5-0ef1c288cefc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126933" :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $r(t)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{a(t)\\,i+b(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\nr(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${r}^{′}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${r}^{′}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}]}
